# Frontend configuration. Copy to .env and edit; .env is gitignored.
#
#   cp frontend/.env.example frontend/.env
#
# compose.yaml reads this file and then .env over the top of it, the same way
# it reads the backend's pair.
#
# READ THE SCOPES BELOW BEFORE SETTING ANYTHING. Unlike the backend, where every
# variable is read by the running process, these belong to three different
# stages, and a value set in the wrong one fails in silence rather than loudly.
#
#   1. HOST DEV    read by `npm run dev`. The container has no Vite in it.
#   2. BUILD       read while Vite bundles. Frozen into the output; changing it
#                  on a running container does nothing at all.
#   3. RUNTIME     read by nginx when the container starts.

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. HOST DEV — `npm run dev` only
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where the dev server proxies /api. Set it when the backend is not on
# localhost:3000 — another port, a container, a teammate's machine.
#
# No VITE_ prefix, deliberately: only VITE_* variables reach the browser, and
# this one configures the dev server itself.
#DEV_API_TARGET=http://localhost:3000

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. BUILD — static-host deployments only
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leave this UNSET for local work and for the Docker image. Both put a proxy in
# front — Vite in development, nginx in the image — so the app stays on one
# origin and uses its '/api' default.
#
# Set it only when you deploy dist/ to a static host, where nothing proxies and
# the API needs its absolute URL. Vite inlines it while bundling, so such a
# deployment is built for one API and cannot be repointed without rebuilding.
# compose.yaml passes it as a build arg, not as an environment variable.
#
# Know the cost before choosing this path. Leaving one origin means:
#
#   * CORS becomes load-bearing. WEB_ORIGIN on the API must list this app's
#     origin exactly — scheme, host and port.
#   * The refresh cookie is SameSite=Lax by default. Lax still travels between
#     ports and subdomains of ONE registrable domain, so api.example.com with
#     app.example.com is fine. It is NOT sent between genuinely different
#     sites, so a frontend on example.pages.dev with an API on example.com
#     silently fails to refresh: the user is signed out when the access token
#     expires, with nothing in the console to say why. That combination needs
#     COOKIE_SAMESITE=None on the API, which needs real HTTPS on both ends.
#
#VITE_API_BASE=https://api.ilham.example

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. RUNTIME — the container
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where nginx forwards /api. compose.yaml sets this to the `api` service, which
# is what it should be there, and that setting wins over this file. Put a value
# here only when you run the image outside compose.
#
# nginx resolves the name for each request, so the backend can restart on a new
# address without stranding the proxy on the old one.
#API_UPSTREAM=http://api:3000
